t industry so  they're constantly battling global  competition and in in order to have  these copyright laws you would have to  have a powerful state that is respected  in the global international community  and of course the Islamic Republic of  Iran has had tense moments with many  folks in the world and so at the same  time even friends as well as foes I have  have sort of pushed the the carpet  industry in some ways to become  independent but if there the tension  lies where the merchants want to have an  independence independence in terms of  the carpet industry while the state  wants to control the carpet industry now  if you if they were to give power to the  state the state might be able to control  the copyright issues but again copyright  issues are incredibly difficult for for  anybody so rug merchants know that when  they commissioned a designer to come up  with a design they probably have at most  a year or two unless the design gets  stolen and then they have to come up  with new designs and these are sort of  high-end merchants so these are more  like sort of luxury boutique type  carpets so these are carpets that that  they know will only last for such a  short time so a carpet takes depends on  the size and the intricacies of its of  the details of the design but it takes  maybe about a year for it to be woven  and it takes about six months before  that to come up with a design the colors  and the scheme and so by the time the  color the carpet hits the market they  know that now they have to work on a new  design so the the the very best carpet  producers are at the forefront of making  sure they are coming up with new designs  which is very difficult and very arduous  and within your on there they're copying  their carpets but they're also copying  their carpets in India and Pakistan in  Egypt in Turkey in Morocco in China and  it used to be sometimes machine-made but  now they are making handmade Persian  carpets in China so it is incredibly  dif